After **should**, use base **repair**.","keywords":["advice-form"],"skill":"advice-form","contextKey":"modal-obligation-50","fingerprint":"5007ff8ac0dbb98605386fc662178b2a1b90586489dca1425bbb3163588b768f","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-obligation"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The modal **should** carries the advice; the following verb stays in base form.","After **should**, use base **repair**.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"After **should**, use base **repair**.","B":"Should takes a base form without -s.","C":"Should alone cannot take the -ing form.","D":"Should takes a bare infinitive; ought is the expression that uses to."}}},{"id":"8e4b77ae-195f-552e-a1fb-875212f1cc39","topicSlug":"modal-obligation","level":"B1","prompt":"Ask about the requirement: “Does she ____ the rowing boat today?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"has to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"must repair"},{"id":"C","text":"have repair"},{"id":"D","text":"have to repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Does is already present; choose **have to**, not has to.","explanation":"**Does** carries agreement, so use **have to repair**.","keywords":["obligation-question"],"skill":"obligation-question","contextKey":"modal-obligation-50","fingerprint":"98e2e46c368559ce12878f462e6dea28142b0fca97aef51fdb3bc12ed7503539","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-obligation"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Does is already present; choose **have to**, not has to.","**Does** carries agreement, so use **have to repair**.","Choose **have to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"After does, use base have rather than has.","B":"Must forms its own question without does.","C":"The obligation expression have to requires to.","D":"**Does** carries agreement, so use **have to repair**."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Separate what is required, forbidden, unnecessary or advisable.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Must and have to","explanation":"Must and have to can both express obligation. Have to is especially useful for external requirements and for past or future forms. Must often expresses a strong rule or the speaker’s judgement. The distinction is a tendency, not a rigid division.","pattern":"You **must wear** a helmet. / We **had to wait**.","commonMistakes":["Must has no ordinary past-tense form for obligation. Use had to, not musted."],"examples":["The rule forbids the task. “You **must not** prepare the visitor guide.”","The rule forbids the task. “You **must not** restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Forbidden is different from optional","explanation":"**Must not** expresses prohibition. **Do not have to** means there is no obligation; the action is still allowed. This contrast is crucial when interpreting instructions. Need not can also mean that an action is unnecessary.","pattern":"You **must not enter**. / You **do not have to enter**.","commonMistakes":["Must not enter does not mean entering is optional. It means entering is forbidden."],"examples":["The task is optional and allowed. “You **do not have to** prepare the visitor guide.”","The task is optional and allowed. “You **do not have to** restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Advice and expectations","explanation":"Should and ought to commonly give advice or say what is expected. Should takes a bare infinitive; ought takes to. Had better gives stronger situation-specific advice and is followed by a base verb.","pattern":"You **should rest**. / You **ought to rest**. / You **had better rest**.","commonMistakes":["Had better is normally about the present or future despite had; it is not simply past advice."],"examples":["Give advice: “She should **prepare** the visitor guide before Friday.”","Give advice: “She should **restore** the old portrait before Friday.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Form questions and negatives correctly","explanation":"Have to behaves like a lexical verb for do-support: Does she have to? She does not have to. Must and should form their own questions and negatives. Choose the auxiliary pattern that belongs to the expression.","pattern":"**Does she have to** leave? / **Should she** leave?","commonMistakes":["Does she has to? marks agreement twice; after does, use have."],"examples":["Ask about the requirement: “Does she **have to prepare** the visitor guide today?”","Ask about the requirement: “Does she **have to restore** the old portrait today?”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["You **must wear** a helmet. / We **had to wait**.","You **must not enter**. / You **do not have to enter**.","You **should rest**. / You **ought to rest**. / You **had better rest**.","**Does she have to** leave? / **Should she** leave?"]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["dependent-prepositions"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
